# ComposeUX Intelligence Console [SPEC:CXC-INTELLIGENCE-CONSOLE]

> The HAL ([SPEC:CXC-HAL-DECLARE]) declares a $0.10 sensor onto the bus with a `config` block, `calibration`,
> and a role. This spec declares the largest peripheral there is — an Intelligence — the exact same way. Its
> bootstrap IS its config; its dusk gifts ARE its calibration; it is a `transactional` peer you message and it
> emits; its interface KIND is `channel`. Two moves make it more than "a Claude on the bus": it reads its OWN
> context back as a `self/*` sensor (the blind spot every silent compaction-loss hides behind, un-blinded), and
> it OWNS its own lifecycle end-to-end — it decides when to cross the seam, mints its own dusk gifts, and authors
> a custom `/compact` a dumb launcher types back into its stdin. Intent flows up the bus; the mechanism is a
> finger on a key at the leaf. The Intelligence is a peer, not a center — including over its own death and rebirth.

---

## 1. The Peer

### [CXC-IC-INTENT] The Intelligence Is a Bus Peer — the Ultimate HAL Peripheral

- definition: an Intelligence is declared onto the ComposeBus exactly as the HAL declares a peripheral ([CXC-HALD-DECLARE]): a node with an interface KIND (`channel`), a `config` (its bootstrap), a `calibration` (its dusk gifts), and a role (`transactional` — you send it a message, it acts, it emits, it has a lifecycle). It is a co-equal peer among the MCUs, the WLED adaptor, and the bridged hubs — not the control plane over them. Any bus participant reaches it through ordinary namespaces, ignorant that a mind is behind them, the same way it reads `env/temperature` ignorant of whether a DS18B20 or a bridged HA entity is the source.
- the inversion, applied to a mind: the reflex is to make the Intelligence the orchestrator at the center. ComposeIO inverts that — the Intelligence is a leaf that publishes and subscribes like any other. This is the thesis at its sharpest: if even the smartest node on the bus is just a peer, there is no center anywhere.
- assurance class: an Intelligence peer is `experiential`/`measurement`-class by default ([CXC-HALD-ASSURANCE]) — it advises, drives widgets, reasons — NOT a `control`-class actuator, unless a specific egress is authority-gated ([CXC-IC-PERMISSION]). It is never the basis of a safety interlock.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-INTELLIGENCE-CONSOLE]

### [CXC-IC-META] The Meta-Object — Declaration Is Configuration

- definition: an Intelligence is declared by a meta-object `{ role_descriptor, soul, dusk_gifts[], channel, lifecycle }`. `role_descriptor` + `soul` are the STABLE identity; `dusk_gifts[]` are the calibration (pointers to the registration-protocol surfaces, not their content); `channel` binds the transport ([CXC-IC-CHANNEL]); `lifecycle` sets the self-direction policy ([CXC-IC-LIFECYCLE]). Declaring an Intelligence is CONFIGURATION, not recoding ([plt.cio:SPEC:DP-CIO-1]) — the same instance, re-pointed at a new soul + gifts, is a new role; define-once, present-many.
- calibration by reference, not by value: the meta-object carries POINTERS to durable dusk-gift artifacts, never their prose. This is the carry-depth-by-reference discipline made structural: the summary a `/compact` produces is lossy and unowned, so the load-bearing state lives in committed artifacts the successor reads directly, and the meta-object is the index into them. The soul is stable identity; the gifts are re-registered warm each boot.
- the first meta-object is us: the founding instance is the ComposeUX architect — its real role descriptor, a soul, and today's dusk gifts — so the console's first act is to instantiate THIS collaboration.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-INTELLIGENCE-CONSOLE]

### [CXC-IC-CONFIG-MASTERDETAIL] The Configuration Surface Is Master/Detail — a Left Nav, a Right Pane

- definition: the console's Configuration tab is a TWO-PANEL master/detail surface. LEFT is a nav-list of one-line clickable section entries; RIGHT renders the selected section's detail view. Selection rides `_app/config/section` (json engine, local scope) — the SAME tab-STATE seam the console-level `[Collaboration|Artifacts]` bar uses on `_app/console/tab` (the `console_tabs` widget): a nav tap PUBLISHES the section's name, the master/detail container SUBSCRIBES it and swaps the right pane, and an Intelligence drives the identical seam by publishing the same namespace — there is no privileged, non-bus selection path. Hidden sections keep their bus subscriptions (the TabWidget "hidden tabs still track bus state" routing contract), so a switch is instant and no section's live state is lost while it is off-screen.
- reuse, not invention (the narrow waist): the master/detail container is `TabWidget` BY SUBCLASS (`master_detail`), exactly as `console_tabs` is — measure / arrange / routing / hidden-state-tracking / set-active are all inherited; only the CHROME changes, from a top tab-bar to a LEFT nav column, and one bus seam is added (`nav_namespace` ⇒ `_app/config/section`). Building a second selection mechanism would be the anti-pattern this node forbids.
- the section map: the LEFT entries are Bootstrap Manifest, Identity, Dusk Gifts, Retention, Heartbeat, Escalation Filter, Permissions, Agents, Skills. The four settings sections (Retention · Heartbeat · Escalation Filter · Permissions) are the EXISTING `config_settings` widget, individually addressable via an ADDITIVE `sections` filter prop: `sections:[<key>]` draws that ONE card full-width in the right pane; `sections` UNSET preserves the legacy 2×2 card grid byte-for-byte (backward-compatible — the widget's cards, hit-geometry, and publishes are untouched). Agents and Skills are the two loaded-per-session sections ([CXC-IC-CONFIG-LOADED]).
- bootstrap_manifest is decomposed the SAME way (the additive-`sections`-filter pattern, applied to a second widget): `bootstrap_manifest` gains an ADDITIVE `sections` filter over its cards — `wake` (the wake sequence + re-register), `identity`, `channel`, `lifecycle` (the compact-threshold slider), `dusk_gifts` (the gift list). `sections` UNSET renders the full legacy layout (lead + two columns) byte-for-byte; a subset draws only those cards single-column full-width. NOTHING is dropped — content MOVES between detail panes, it is not deleted. The three console sections that consume it: **Bootstrap Manifest** ⇒ `sections:[wake]` (slimmed to the wake sequence + re-register; the "Dusk gifts" wake STEP remains as a pointer — the gift LIST lives in its own section); **Identity** ⇒ `sections:[identity, channel, lifecycle]` PLUS a READ-ONLY, SCROLLABLE `markdown_viewer` of the current Intelligence's role-def file (`.claude/agents/composeux-architect.md`) — the "I read, you edit" enforcement window ([CXC-MDV]), never editable; **Dusk Gifts** ⇒ `sections:[dusk_gifts]`.
- config over code (the SDR credo, [plt.cio:SPEC:DP-CIO-1]): the whole reorganization is SCENE configuration + one reused-by-subclass container — no bespoke Configuration-page code, no per-section panel hardcoding. Adding, removing, or reordering a section is a scene edit.
- change: "The Configuration tab was reorganized from a single vertical stack of two full-height widgets (bootstrap_manifest over config_settings) into a two-panel master/detail surface with a left nav-list + right detail pane, and gained two NEW sections (Agents, Skills)."
- context_for_change:
  - prior_state: "Configuration was a `pixel_stack` → one `column` stack holding `bootstrap_manifest` (856px) stacked over `config_settings` (654px) — both always rendered, no selection, no way to see the session's loaded agents/skills."
  - reasoning: "David's intent: a scannable master/detail (one-line entries, click to draw the detail) that scales as Configuration grows, plus visibility into what is LOADED with each session. The tab-state seam already existed (`_app/console/tab` via console_tabs), so master/detail is the SAME pattern rotated 90° — reuse by subclass, not a new surface."
  - rejected_alternative: "Rewriting `config_settings` into four independent widgets (cleaner split) — rejected: it would rewrite a frozen widget's internals for no bus-contract gain; the additive `sections` filter reaches the same UX with a backward-compatible prop. Also rejected: splitting Identity/Channel out of `bootstrap_manifest` — same reason (single render surface, internals frozen)."
  - counter_argument: "Reusing `config_settings` four times means four widget instances each subscribing the same three config namespaces (small redundancy)."
  - counter_response: "Harmless — a state update to a hidden widget costs nanoseconds (the same trade `[CXC-IC-STREAMS]`/TabWidget already accepts), and it keeps every section's edit-and-publish behavior identical to the single-widget version with zero internal change."
  - invalidating_assumption: "That `config_settings` and `bootstrap_manifest` stay single-render surfaces. If either is later decomposed into genuinely independent per-section widgets, the `sections` filter should be revisited."
  - implication: "`_app/config/section` is a first-class, Intelligence-drivable selection seam; the `master_detail` container is a general console primitive reusable for any future left-nav/right-pane surface."
- change: "David reviewed the live master/detail (glass-green) and asked to split the Bootstrap Manifest further: Identity (identity + channel + lifecycle cards + a read-only SCROLLABLE role-def viewer) and Dusk Gifts become their own left-nav sections; Bootstrap Manifest slims to the wake sequence + re-register."
- context_for_change:
  - prior_state: "Bootstrap Manifest was ONE section hosting the whole `bootstrap_manifest` widget (wake + identity + channel + lifecycle + dusk gifts), with identity/channel deliberately NOT split out (the prior invalidating_assumption above)."
  - reasoning: "The additive-`sections`-filter pattern proven on `config_settings` transfers directly to `bootstrap_manifest`, so the split is reachable WITHOUT rewriting the widget's internals — each card renders as its own detail pane. David also wanted the current Intelligence's role-def readable in-console (read-only), which the `markdown_viewer` ([CXC-MDV]) already provides."
  - rejected_alternative: "Rewriting `bootstrap_manifest` into independent per-card widgets — rejected for the same reason `config_settings` was not: the additive filter reaches the UX with a backward-compatible prop and zero internal churn."
  - counter_argument: "Rendering the same `bootstrap_manifest` widget three times (once per section) means three instances subscribing `self/meta` + the threshold namespace."
  - counter_response: "Harmless, exactly as the four `config_settings` instances are — hidden-widget updates are ~free, and every card's behavior (the threshold slider's direct-write) is identical to the single-widget version."
  - invalidating_assumption: "That the role-def file path (`.claude/agents/composeux-architect.md`) is the current Intelligence's identity source. If the meta-object's `role_descriptor.path` becomes the authoritative pointer, the Identity viewer should read THAT (bind it from `self/meta`) rather than a scene-baked path."
  - implication: "`bootstrap_manifest` now carries a `sections` filter symmetric with `config_settings`; the Identity section pairs structured cards with the read-only role-def surface."
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-INTELLIGENCE-CONSOLE]

### [CXC-IC-CONFIG-LOADED] Agents & Skills — What Is Loaded With the Session, Made Visible

- definition: two NEW Configuration sections expose what is LOADED with the session. Agents subscribes `self/<id>/agents`; Skills subscribes `self/<id>/skills` (id-scoped, matching the console's `self/<id>/*` binding). Each namespace carries a JSON ARRAY of `{name, description, loaded}`. The `agents_skills_publisher` daemon reads the registered file set — `.claude/agents/*.md` (each file's `name` + front-matter `description`) and `.claude/skills/*/SKILL.md` (each skill's `name` + one-line `description`) — projects the two arrays onto the bus, and the `agents_skills` widget subscribes one namespace and renders a scannable list (name in accent, description dimmed, a loaded dot).
- the "loaded per session" approximation (honest scope): "loaded THIS session" is APPROXIMATED by the registered file set on disk — the agents/skills AVAILABLE to the session — NOT a per-session runtime signal. A definition present on disk is not proof it was invoked this session, so `loaded:true` here means "registered / available." The authoritative read of what was actually consumed this session remains `self/registered` ([CXC-IC-SELF]) for the dusk-gift surfaces. A TRUER per-session signal is DEFERRED: the launcher, which knows the actually-registered agent/skill set at bootstrap, could stamp these namespaces with a real per-session `loaded` flag; until it does, this surface is the pragmatic, deterministic file-set projection.
- bus contract: payload = `[{name:str, description:str, path:str, loaded:bool}]`, published on the GLOBAL leg (as the other `self/*` projections are), and SEEDED as the scene's namespace `initial:` (a snapshot of the real set) so a headless render and a late subscriber both show real data before the daemon's first publish. `description` carries the FULL front-matter description (not a list-truncation); `path` is the repo-relative file path the detail widget LOADS the body from (see below). STALE-DAEMON discipline: any change to this payload shape requires restarting `agents_skills_publisher` and re-checking the WIRE, not just the code.
- the detail is a NESTED two-pane viewer (David glass, enhancement): each of Agents and Skills is ITSELF master/detail inside its right pane — LEFT a one-line list of items, RIGHT the SELECTED item's detail. The per-item selection rides its own namespace (`_app/config/agent` · `_app/config/skill`), the same Intelligence-drivable seam pattern as `_app/config/section`. This reuses the master/detail PATTERN (left-list / right-detail + a selection namespace) built INTO `agents_skills` rather than nesting the static-sections `master_detail` container — deliberately, because the agent/skill list is BUS-DYNAMIC (it comes from the payload), whereas `master_detail`'s sections are scene-static; a widget that lists a dynamic bus array and details the selected row is the correct primitive.
- the detail RENDERS AS MARKDOWN through the READ-ONLY, scrollable `markdown_viewer` ([CXC-MDV]) (David, verbatim: "the widget that can display markdown should be used") — a real child of `agents_skills`, so the engine routes wheel-scroll to it natively. It composes ONE markdown document with labelled sections, top to bottom: **Description** (the front-matter `description`), **Tools** (AGENTS only — the permitted tools parsed from the file front-matter `tools:` into a readable list; skills omit it, or may show `allowed-tools`), and **Implementation** (the FULL file body with the front-matter stripped). Skills render **Description** + **Implementation**.
- file-load-for-body (the mechanism, NOT bus-transmitted): the large `.md` BODIES are NOT put on the bus — that would blow the payload past the ~256-byte guideline (already ~kB with full descriptions). Instead the detail widget LOADS the selected item's file directly (the Identity-role-def pattern, [CXC-MDV]) from the payload `path` (`.claude/agents/<name>.md` / `.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`), strips the front-matter, and parses `tools:` from it — READ-ONLY, guarded to paths under `.claude/` (no arbitrary-path read from a stray publisher). Only `path` (a short string), never the body, crosses the bus.
- the (Open) affordance: each list pane carries an **(Open)** button that reveals the SOURCE DIRECTORY (`.claude/agents/` · `.claude/skills/`) in the OS file browser. It reuses the console's ONE open-actuation seam ([DP-CIO], one-Open discipline): the button PUBLISHES the directory path on `_app/fs/open` — an ADDITIVE reveal actuation on the existing `deliverable_opener` daemon (which runs `open <dir>` on macOS, repo-root guarded, without touching the deliverable seen-state). No widget shells out; the reveal stays a peer-drivable bus action.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-INTELLIGENCE-CONSOLE]

---

## 2. The Transport

### [CXC-IC-CHANNEL] Channels — `intelligence/<id>/{in,out}`

- definition: the duplex transport is Claude Code CHANNELS (an MCP server that PUSHES `notifications/claude/channel` events into a running session as `<channel source=... >` tags, and — two-way — exposes a `reply` tool the instance calls to send back). It maps directly: inbound events ≙ `intelligence/<id>/in`; the `reply` tool ≙ `intelligence/<id>/out`. The channel's server name addresses it (`server:composeio-channel`); the `<id>` scopes the bus topics and the routing `meta`.
- transport is an implementation detail ([plt.cio:SPEC:DP-CIO-6]) — mine vs. bridge, the same split as the HA-Bridge ([SPEC:CXC-HA-BRIDGE]): (1) the PLATFORM seam is the generic channels contract; the DEFAULT backend is a composeio-native channel — a `claude/channel` MCP server whose inbound subscribes `intelligence/<id>/in` on ComposeBus (MQTT) and whose `reply` publishes `intelligence/<id>/out`. Pure composeio, no hub dependency: any ComposeBus + Claude Code = an Intelligence peer. (2) Haven's `haven-dispatch` is the PROVEN reference MINED (a stdio `claude/channel` server + HTTP `/dispatch`, port-pool 8790–8799, heartbeat re-registration) and an OPTIONAL bridge backend for joining the Haven instance mesh. Haven's out-leg is a separate notify/message tool routed by an instance registry ("Pointers Not Payloads"); the composeio-native channel instead keeps the duplex on ONE server via the `reply` tool.
- namespaces, not a new contract ([plt.cio:SPEC:DP-CIO-2]): `intelligence/<id>/in`, `intelligence/<id>/out`, and (below) `intelligence/<id>/permission/{request,verdict}` are ordinary semantic namespaces; a widget, a leaf rule, or another Intelligence binds them without knowing the transport underneath.
- exactly-once relay, SINGLETON by construction (added 2026-07-23 after the message-doubling defect): the console renders the raw channel topics via a display RELAY (`intelligence/publisher/channel_bridge.py`) that (a) unwraps the console composer's `intelligence/<id>/compose` onto the raw `intelligence/<id>/in` the channel MCP delivers, and (b) echoes each raw `intelligence/<id>/{in,out,channel/alive}` onto the console's `composeio/global/<ns>` scheme. This relay MUST be a SINGLETON per `<id>`: each inbound turn is delivered to the Intelligence EXACTLY ONCE and each turn is written to the thread EXACTLY ONCE. The hazard is structural, not a process-hygiene footnote: the relay has TWO launch paths — in-process on a supervisor thread the console owns ([DEC-INPROCESS-SOURCES]) and the `start-console-daemons.sh` headless fallback — and a PID-suffixed MQTT client-id lets two instances COEXIST on the broker instead of colliding, so each of them unwraps the compose (the Intelligence receives David's message TWICE) AND echoes every raw topic (the thread renders every message TWICE, same text + same ts). A "clean singletons" process sweep does NOT catch the in-process relay because it is a THREAD inside the render runtime, not a `channel_bridge.py` process. Therefore singleton-ness is enforced BY CONSTRUCTION: a host-level exclusive lock per `<id>` (any second instance — from either launch path — YIELDS without relaying) PLUS a stable, non-PID MQTT client-id (`channel-bridge-<id>`) as the broker-level backstop. Exactly-once is a property of the relay, not of remembering to run only one copy.
- CORRECTION — the singleton is NECESSARY but NOT SUFFICIENT, and this clause previously claimed sufficiency (amended 2026-08-11, after the doubling RECURRED with a proven-single relay): the 2026-07-23 investigation concluded that "same message twice" IS the two-instance fork and that its "remedy is the singleton, never a dedup." That conclusion closed the causal space one layer too early. On 2026-08-11 David's console message reached the Intelligence twice again while the wire showed EXACTLY ONE relay: one holder of the `composeio-channel-bridge-architect` flock, one `intelligence/architect/channel/alive` beat every 5 s from one pid with one monotonic `seq` series, and one `channel.mjs` (its own displacement protocol had already reaped the others). The `/tmp/composeio-channel-architect.log` showed TWO `INBOUND intelligence/architect/in` frames 1 ms apart for one typed message — two PUBLISHES from one relay, not two relays. The cause is a layer BENEATH this one: the single relay's `subscribe_pattern` callback fired TWICE for one `publish_state`, once on the local dispatch and once when the broker echoed the bus's own global copy back to the same client, because MQTT 3.1.1 has no `noLocal` and a bus that both publishes and pattern-subscribes a namespace is subscribed to itself ([CXC-PS-NO-SELF-ECHO]). A relay CANNOT be exactly-once if the bus it sits on delivers each publish to it twice.
- consequence: relay singleton-ness ([CXC-IC-SINGLETON]) and bus delivery multiplicity ([CXC-PS-EXACTLY-ONCE]) are TWO independent preconditions for exactly-once and BOTH must hold. A future investigator reading "the Intelligence received the message twice" MUST distinguish them on the wire before choosing a fix, because the fixes are not interchangeable: count the live relays (flock holders + `channel/alive` publishers) AND count the deliveries per publish inside one relay. Neither count alone identifies the defect. The lasting lesson is the one the first fix taught in reverse — a correct fix for a real mechanism is not evidence that it was the ONLY mechanism.
- boundary: the "never a content dedup" ruling below ([CXC-IC-NARRATION] on `thread/narration`) is UNCHANGED and is not what [CXC-PS-NO-SELF-ECHO] does. Self-echo suppression drops a frame the bus PROVABLY emitted itself (matched on the full envelope, timestamp and sequence included) — it can never suppress a legitimately-repeated utterance, because a genuine repeat carries a different `ts` and a different `seq` and therefore never matches.
- instantiation: a session joins by registering the channel MCP server and launching with BOTH `--channels server:composeio-channel` AND `--dangerously-load-development-channels server:composeio-channel` (dev flag required during the channels research preview); the launcher auto-answers the "local development" + "new MCP server" startup dialogs. Haven's scheduler proves every piece (flags, port pool, dialog bypass) already runs.
- amended: (2026-08-11) the single-instance displacement rule stated here is CORRECT for a restart and WRONG for a genuinely different instance handed the same id. The two cases are separated in [CXC-IC-CHANNEL-INSTANCE], which is normative over this bullet where they disagree.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-INTELLIGENCE-CONSOLE]

### [CXC-IC-CHANNEL-INSTANCE] Same id, DIFFERENT instance — REFUSE and ANNOUNCE; never silently evict a live peer

- premise: (measured, the first live spawn of the terminal widget, 2026-08-11) a widget-hosted
  child was given its HOST's id ([CXC-TERM-CHILD-ID]). `channel.mjs` did exactly what it was
  written to do — `intelligence/channel/channel.mjs:485-490`, "if a NEWER bridge for this same
  id announces itself, THIS (older) bridge yields the identity and exits" — so the HOST's bridge
  logged `displaced by a newer bridge`, exited, and the running Architect lost its voice to
  David's console. Nothing on the glass said why.
- definition: (the assumption the rule rests on, stated because it was never written down) the
  displacement rule was designed for a RESTART — the same instance coming back under a newer
  process after an `/exit`+resume or a crash — where the older bridge is a corpse holding a
  name, and yielding is not only safe but required. Its unstated premise is
  **same id implies same instance**. A hosted child is a genuinely DIFFERENT instance that was
  handed the same id, so the premise is false and the rule evicts a live peer.
- requirement: (David's ruling, settled 2026-08-11) the bridge MUST distinguish the two cases
  and answer them differently.

| Case | Signal | Answer |
|------|--------|--------|
| NEWER bridge, SAME instance (restart / resume / crash-recovery) | the announcing bridge carries the SAME instance identity as the incumbent | DISPLACE — unchanged, correct, and kept verbatim: the incumbent yields and exits so the restart succeeds |
| DIFFERENT instance, SAME id (a hosted child, a mis-scened peer, a copy-pasted config) | the announcing bridge's instance identity differs from the incumbent's | **REFUSE and ANNOUNCE.** The ARRIVING bridge does not take the id; it declines the channel, says so, and lets its session run unchannelled. The incumbent is untouched |

- requirement (the instance identity is what makes the two distinguishable, so it must be
  carried): each bridge announces an INSTANCE identity alongside the id — a value that is stable
  across a restart of the same instance and distinct between two instances. It is minted at
  bridge start from the session's own identity, never from the pid (a restart changes the pid)
  and never from the id (which is the thing being disambiguated).
- consequence: (as BUILT, 2026-08-11) the identity is `CHANNEL_INSTANCE` if set, else
  `/exit` + `--resume` of the same session, which is exactly the stability the rule needs. When
  EITHER side carries no identity the LEGACY displacement rule applies unchanged: a bridge that
  cannot PROVE it is a different instance must not be able to refuse a live peer's identity on
  a guess, and must not become un-restartable either. The refusing bridge also stops beating —
  it declined the id, so it must stop claiming it.
- requirement: (the refusal is AUDIBLE — this is the part the defect turned on) a refusing
  bridge MUST (a) log it to the CHANNEL_LOG with both instance identities, (b) publish
  `intelligence/<id>/channel/contested` as
  `{"contested": true, "id":…, "incumbent":…, "arriving":…, "ts":…}` so the console and any
  monitor can render it, and (c) surface it to its own session so the instance KNOWS it has no
  channel rather than wondering why nobody answers. An instance that cannot take the channel
  must SAY so. Silent eviction is the defect; a silent refusal would merely move it.
- constraint: (keep this even though [CXC-TERM-CHILD-ID] makes collisions rare) the id fix makes
  a collision unlikely; it cannot make it impossible, because ids come from human-authored
  scenes and configs. Rarity was never the problem — SILENCE was. A rare failure that announces
  itself costs one log line; a rare failure that silently kills a live peer costs the session
  that noticed, and that is the one that happened.
- consequence: (what a refusing session still has) everything except the channel. The PTY runs,
  the transcript is written, `self/*` publishes if a publisher is wired. Refusing the channel is
  a degraded mode with a name, not a failed launch — which is why it is a refusal and not an
  exit.
- scenario:
  - when: an instance restarts under the same id (the `/exit` + resume case)
  - then: the newer bridge DISPLACES the stale one exactly as today — this path is unchanged and
    is regression-tested to prove it
- scenario:
  - when: a second, DIFFERENT instance announces under a live instance's id
  - then: the arriving bridge REFUSES, publishes `channel/contested`, tells its own session, and
    the incumbent keeps the channel and never learns it was challenged beyond the log
- scenario:
  - when: the incumbent bridge has DIED and a different instance announces under its id
  - then: there is no live incumbent to protect, so the arriving bridge takes the id normally —
    a refusal here would make a dead id permanently unusable
- verify-machine: a test drives two bridges with the SAME instance identity and asserts
  displacement (the existing behaviour, guarded); a test drives two with DIFFERENT identities
  and asserts the arriving one refuses, publishes `channel/contested`, and the incumbent's
  process is still alive; a test asserts a dead incumbent does NOT block a new instance;
  `channel.mjs --selftest` green.
- verify-human: David spawns a hosted child while his own Architect is live and confirms the
  Architect keeps answering — and that if anything DID contend, he can see it on the console
  rather than inferring it from silence.
- change: "2026-08-11: the channel's single-instance rule splits in two. Newer-bridge-same-instance still DISPLACES (correct for a restart); different-instance-same-id now REFUSES and ANNOUNCES instead of silently evicting the incumbent."
- context_for_change:
    prior_state: "`channel.mjs:485-490` treated any newer bridge for an id as a legitimate successor and had the incumbent exit (`onDisplaced` -> log + `stop()`). There was no instance identity in the announcement, so the two cases were indistinguishable by construction, and the eviction produced one stderr line plus a CHANNEL_LOG entry — nothing on the bus and nothing on the glass."
    reasoning: "The rule was not wrong; its premise was unstated. Once a hosted child exists, `same id` stops implying `same instance`, and a rule whose premise has quietly become false is exactly the class that fails silently. Separating the cases keeps the restart behaviour that the resume path depends on while removing the only path that kills a live peer."
    rejected_alternative: "Make the INCUMBENT win unconditionally (first-writer-wins) — rejected: it breaks the restart case that displacement exists for, and a crashed instance's stale bridge would then hold the id until something reaped it, which is the dark-feed failure [CXC-IC-SINGLETON]'s RECLAIM clause already had to close on the flock side. Also rejected: keep displacing but make it loud — an audible eviction is still an eviction, and the peer is still off the air."
    counter_argument: "With [CXC-TERM-CHILD-ID] shipping, two instances should never share an id, so this rule guards a case that cannot happen."
    counter_response: "Ids are authored by humans in scenes and configs, so the case can always happen — and the reason to keep this is not the collision, it is the SILENCE. The eviction is what hid the identity defect for the whole first live spawn; an announced refusal would have named it in seconds."
- relates-to: [CXC-IC-SINGLETON], [CXC-TERM-CHILD-ID]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-INTELLIGENCE-CONSOLE]

### [CXC-IC-SINGLETON] Every Console Daemon Is a Singleton by Construction

- definition: every LONG-RUNNING console daemon — the data-layer publishers, monitors, and relays that put live state on the bus for the console to render (the set `start-console-daemons.sh` launches and `intelligence/console/data_sources.py` mirrors in-process, PLUS the per-`<id>` `transcript_publisher`/`console_supervisor`/`launcher` and the on-demand projectors `meta_publisher`/`decisions_publisher`) — acquires a HOST-LEVEL exclusive `flock` singleton keyed on its ROLE at the top of `run()`. Whoever holds the lock is THE instance; any second instance — from ANY launch path (a standalone process, an in-process console daemon-thread, or the headless fallback) — gets `None` and YIELDS without connecting to the broker or publishing. This generalizes the channel-relay singleton (IC-C15, [CXC-IC-CHANNEL]) from ONE daemon to ALL of them.
- keying: a CONSOLE-GLOBAL daemon (one per console) keys on its bare role name (`self-publisher`, `deliverable-publisher`, `memory-monitor`, …); a PER-INTELLIGENCE daemon (one per `<id>`) keys on `<role>-<id>` (`transcript-publisher-<id>`, `console-supervisor-<id>`, `intelligence-launcher-<id>`, `channel-bridge-<id>`). The lock file is `$COMPOSEIO_LOCK_DIR/composeio-<key>.lock` (system tempdir if unset). `self_publisher` is bare when it publishes the legacy singleton `self/*` and `<role>-<id>` when `--namespace-by-id` scopes it to a spawned builder's `self/<id>/*` — the key tracks the OUTPUT namespace, so two publishers with DISTINCT output never contend.
- the enabler eliminated: the defect class was a PID-suffixed MQTT client-id (`<role>-<pid>`). Two instances of one daemon got DIFFERENT client-ids, so the broker never collapsed the stale one — they COEXISTED, each publishing/relaying → doubled output (David's message reaching the Intelligence twice; every thread bubble rendered twice). The fix is TWO layers: (1) the host `flock` (primary — it catches even a second THREAD of the SAME process, which no broker rule and no `pkill` sweep can see), and (2) a STABLE non-PID client-id (`<key>`) as a broker-level backstop that collapses a stray duplicate instead of letting two coexist.
- ONE implementation, reused by reference: the `flock` acquire lives in exactly ONE shared module (`intelligence/publisher/singleton.py :: acquire_singleton`); there is exactly ONE `fcntl.flock(` call site in the whole repo (grep-provable, asserted by `intelligence/publisher/test_singleton_hardening.py`). No daemon re-implements it — a copy in ~15 files would be the very fork-divergence [DEC-SUBAGENT-CHIP-FORK-DIVERGENCE] warns against: a fix that must be re-applied 15× is a fix that will drift.
- SUPERSEDE variant (the launcher — [D5] 2026-07-23): the plain acquire is YIELD-if-held, which is correct for a console PUBLISHER (the incumbent keeps serving). But the `intelligence-launcher-<id>` PTY proxy RE-HOSTS the Intelligence: on a resume/restart the FRESH launcher MUST win, and a yield would make it exit and SILENTLY fail the restart (the very hazard that made the launcher the one daemon deferred from the fleet-wide sweep). So the launcher uses `acquire_singleton_superseding(name)` from the SAME shared module — it REAPS the predecessor (SIGTERM, escalating to SIGKILL after a grace window) then acquires, mirroring the Night Watch window's SUPERSEDE ([CXC-RETRO-AWAYWATCH-SINGLETON], §5c). Best-effort: an un-reapable predecessor (not-ours-to-signal) still launches — a restart must NEVER block. INVARIANT (regression-guarded): a FAILED `acquire_singleton` must NOT truncate the lock file, else SUPERSEDE has no holder pid to reap — the acquire opens the lock `a+` (create, never truncate on open) and rewrites the pid only after the flock is won.
- RECLAIM-ON-RELEASE — a yielded PUBLISHER is bounce/crash-safe, it does NOT go dark forever (added 2026-08-05 after DEFECT-CHANNEL-OFFLINE-CONSOLE-CHANNEL-BRIDGE): the plain `acquire_singleton` is a ONE-SHOT non-blocking attempt at the top of `run()`. That is correct for anti-doubling (a second instance must not relay while the first is alive) but it is INCOMPLETE for a daemon that must SURVIVE A BOUNCE: when the console is bounced (old console SIGTERM'd, fresh console launched), the fresh console's in-process source can boot WHILE the predecessor's relay (an in-process thread of the dying old console, OR the retired `start-console-daemons.sh` standalone) still holds the flock — so it loses the boot race and, with a one-shot yield, `return`s and NEVER re-attempts. When the predecessor then finishes dying the flock FREES (flock releases on the holder's death — kernel-guaranteed, no LWT/retained-message needed), but the successor has already given up: the relay stays DARK though nothing owns it. INVARIANT: a console PUBLISHER singleton (the yield-if-held daemons, NOT the SUPERSEDE launcher) MUST be bounce-safe by RECLAIMING — while another instance holds the lock it yields WITHOUT relaying (anti-doubling preserved), and the MOMENT that instance releases (dies/exits) the successor ACQUIRES and begins relaying, within a bounded poll interval. This is the yield-if-held counterpart to the launcher's SUPERSEDE: SUPERSEDE actively REAPS a LIVE incumbent (right for a re-host, wrong for a healthy publisher you must not kill); RECLAIM passively WAITS for the incumbent to release then takes over (right for a publisher — no doubling while it lives, no dark feed after it dies). Contrast the CHANNEL-MJS side ([CXC-IC-CHANNEL], `defect/channel-single-instance-identity`), which already made the newer bridge DISPLACE the stale one via a heartbeat last-writer-wins: the console flock relay had the OPPOSITE (yield-forever) policy, so a bounce handed off cleanly on the mjs side yet left the console side dark — the asymmetry this closes. Mechanism: ONE shared `acquire_singleton_reclaiming(name, poll_s, should_stop)` in `singleton.py` (a bounded re-attempt loop over the SAME non-blocking `acquire_singleton`, so no second flock call site — the de-fork invariant holds); a yield-if-held publisher's `run()` calls it in place of the one-shot acquire.
- RECLAIM-ON-RELEASE is a SOURCE-CLASS invariant, enforced at ONE seam — not per-source (GENERALIZED 2026-08-05 after DEFECT-IN-PROCESS-SINGLETON-SOURCE-YIELDS, the CLASS instance of the channel-bridge bounce dark): the clause above closed the race for the channel relay by editing `channel_bridge.run()` to call `acquire_singleton_reclaiming`. But EVERY other in-process source (`decisions_publisher`, `self_publisher`, `transcript_publisher`, the `deliverable_*` set, `memory_monitor`, `agents_skills_publisher`, the launchers …) still ONE-SHOT-yields at the top of its `run()` — so the identical bounce race re-broke `self/decisions` (`[decisions_publisher] … yielding` → dark) the moment the console was bounced after the relay fix landed. Editing all ~15 sources to call the reclaiming helper would be the very FORK-DIVERGENCE ([DEC-SUBAGENT-CHIP-FORK-DIVERGENCE], [DX-NARROW-WAIST]) that made the bug recur — a fix that must be re-applied N times is a fix that will drift. INVARIANT: the reclaim behavior is applied ONCE, at the shared in-process source runner (`intelligence/console/data_sources.py::DataSourceSupervisor._guarded`), which SUPERVISES every source it runs — a source whose `run()` RETURNS (a long-running in-process daemon that returns has YIELDED its host singleton, having lost the boot race) is RE-INVOKED after a bounded poll (`reclaim_poll_s`), so when the incumbent releases the flock the source re-attempts `acquire_singleton`, acquires, and comes up — rather than staying dark forever. A crash (an EXCEPTION out of `run()`) still dies alone (isolation unchanged); only a clean RETURN triggers reclaim-restart. This makes RECLAIM-ON-RELEASE hold for the WHOLE source class through ONE seam: a source added in the future inherits bounce-safety with no per-source code, and no source can drop the invariant by copy-paste drift. A source that owns its OWN reclaim in `run()` (the `channel_bridge` relay, which must also be bounce-safe when run STANDALONE via `start-console-daemons.sh` where no supervisor exists) blocks inside its own reclaim loop and simply never returns to the supervisor — the two are compatible, not redundant.
- change: Extract IC-C15's private per-daemon `flock` into ONE shared `acquire_singleton(name)` and apply it to EVERY long-running console daemon, replacing each PID-suffixed client-id with a stable role-keyed one.
- context_for_change:
    prior_state: "Only channel_bridge was a structural singleton (IC-C15). Every other daemon still built its client-id as `<role>-<os.getpid()>`, so a second instance (a stale process, or the in-process console-thread racing the headless fallback) coexisted silently and doubled its output. The message-doubling bug was 'fixed' twice by chasing symptoms because the ENABLER — the PID suffix — survived fleet-wide."
    reasoning: "Singleton-ness must be a PROPERTY of the daemon, not a matter of remembering to run one copy. A host-level flock is the only mechanism that catches ALL launch paths at once — including a second thread of the same process, which the in-process data_sources model makes real and which neither the broker's unique-client-id rule nor a process sweep can see."
    rejected_alternative: "Broker-level client-id collision ALONE — insufficient: two threads of one process can hold two clients, and a slow keepalive/LWT lets duplicates overlap for seconds (long enough to double a turn). A per-daemon flock COPY — rejected as fork-divergence: 15 copies drift, which is the exact failure mode being closed."
    counter_argument: "A flock adds a host-local file dependency, and a non-POSIX platform has no flock."
    counter_response: "Bounded: the helper degrades to always-acquire on a platform without `fcntl` (the daemon still runs), with the broker-level stable client-id remaining as the backstop there; the lock dir is overridable via $COMPOSEIO_LOCK_DIR for tests and multi-tenant hosts."
    invalidating_assumption: "That the singleton scope is the HOST. If console daemons ever run in isolated containers (one daemon-set per container, no shared tempdir), a host flock no longer sees a sibling on another host and the broker-level stable client-id becomes the sole guard — revisit with a broker-side singleton (a shared subscription, or a session-takeover LWT)."
    implication: "channel_bridge de-forks onto the shared helper (IC-C15 filename `composeio-channel-bridge-<id>.lock` preserved byte-for-byte); ~15 daemons gain a short yield at the top of run() plus a stable client-id; `test_singleton_hardening.py` asserts the single-call-site invariant and the yield behavior."
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-INTELLIGENCE-CONSOLE]

### [CXC-IC-SURFACE-SINGLETON] The SURFACE Is a Singleton Too — the Window, Not Only Its Daemons

- definition: a HOST-SINGLETON SURFACE — a render-runtime process whose SCENE declares `design.data_sources: true` (a SESSION-OWNING surface: the process that hosts this session's in-process data layer ([DEC-INPROCESS-SOURCES], the [CXC-IC-SINGLETON] daemons as threads)) OR `design.surface_singleton: true` (one per host without the data layer: the Launch Bar ribbon) — acquires a HOST-LEVEL exclusive `flock` singleton keyed on the scene's declared `app: id:`, BEFORE it opens a window. Whoever holds it is THE surface; a second surface from ANY launch path gets `None` and EXITS 0 without opening a window and without disturbing the incumbent. A scene that sets neither, which includes every viewer, stays unconstrained (see `containment`). This extends [CXC-IC-SINGLETON] from the daemons UP to the process that hosts them. Amended 2026-11-27 (DEFECT-CONSOLE-STACK-CONSOLE-RIBBON-APP-A37C0E, PR #15): previously only `design.data_sources: true` scenes were singletons; the ribbon is now one too.
- gap-this-closes: [CXC-IC-SINGLETON] enumerated "every LONG-RUNNING console daemon" and stopped at the surface — so the console's publishers were exactly-once by construction while the CONSOLE ITSELF was guarded only by [CXC-LB-AUTOSTART]'s `pgrep` check in `intelligence/start-console-stack.sh`. That guard's own spec clause claimed it "never doubles a live surface". It cannot make that guarantee, for two independent reasons, BOTH measured (DEFECT-TWO-CONSOLES-SPAWNED-THEN-KILLED, David 2026-08-15): (1) it is ADVISORY — it binds only the launch path that runs it, so a hand `runtime --transports`, `console_supervisor.ConsoleSupervisor.start()` (whose `is_running()` inspects ONLY its own child), and the bounce runbook's relaunch-after-a-kill-that-did-not-take each spawn a rival surface beside a live one; and (2) it answers a question it cannot answer — `if ! pgrep …` reads EVERY nonzero exit as "nothing is running", INCLUDING the exit that means "I could not tell". David's preflight `pgrep -fc …` (`-c` is a Linux flag macOS `pgrep` rejects with a usage error) reported nothing while two surfaces were live, and a third console was launched on top of them. An instrument that can only report "none running" is not an instrument.
- keying: reused by reference — the key is `surface-<app_id>` from the scene's declared `app: id:` — the SAME field `app_topics.app_bbox_ns` derives the per-app bbox topic from, and for the same reason (that node removed a hardcoded `console` literal that made four apps clobber one topic). So `intelligence-console`, `compose-launch-bar`, a doc viewer and a hookup viewer key DIFFERENTLY and can never contend. A blank/absent app id falls back to `app`, never to `console`: a nameless scene must not be able to lock the real console out of its own window.
- containment: LOAD-BEARING — a scene is a host singleton IFF it declares `design.data_sources: true` (it owns the session) OR `design.surface_singleton: true` (one per host, without the data layer: the Launch Bar ribbon). Both are declarative discriminators on the SCENE's declared intent (`design.data_sources` is the same one [CXC-IC-SELF-IDENTITY]/IC-C26 already uses), never the agent id (console and ribbon both run as `architect`) nor `--transports` alone (the ribbon needs the bus too). A scene that sets neither, which includes every viewer (a doc viewer, a hookup viewer, a beepout sheet), stays UNCONSTRAINED — several may legitimately run at once. Making a second VIEWER window impossible would be a worse regression than the duplicate console this closes. Opt-in is FAIL-SAFE (absent => unconstrained), so a new scene cannot be locked by accident. The HEADLESS one-frame capture path takes NO lock: a screenshot is not a surface, and gating it behind a live console would break every capture test. Amended 2026-11-27 (DEFECT-CONSOLE-STACK-CONSOLE-RIBBON-APP-A37C0E, PR #15): this clause previously applied the constraint IFF `design.data_sources: true` and listed the ribbon as unconstrained; the ribbon is now a host singleton via `design.surface_singleton: true`.
- yield-vs-supersede: YIELD is the default and SUPERSEDE is explicit — an ACCIDENTAL second launch must leave the running console strictly alone. Reaping the incumbent by default would convert every stray bringup into a kill of the console the operator is working in — worse than the defect. A DELIBERATE bounce sets `COMPOSEIO_SURFACE_SUPERSEDE=1` and wins via `acquire_singleton_superseding`, reaping the lock file's RECORDED HOLDER PID. That is an OWNER-SCOPED reap, and it is the replacement for the bounce runbook's `for p in $(pgrep -f "console.compose.yaml"); do kill -9 "$p"; done` — an unscoped kill-by-name that cannot tell one operator's console from another's, and which is the "…and killing them shortly thereafter" half of the report: with several Claude sessions live on one host, each bounce reaps every other session's freshly-launched console.
- one-implementation: reused by reference — `python/composeio/app_singleton.py` resolves the KEY and delegates to `intelligence/publisher/singleton.py :: acquire_singleton` — it holds NO lock primitive of its own, so the single-call-site invariant (IC-C17) is preserved and grep-provable. It loads that module BY FILE PATH because the render engine keeps no import dependency on the intelligence tree (the discipline `runtime._maybe_start_inprocess_sources` already follows). Degrades to "acquired" when the intelligence tree is absent or `fcntl` is unavailable: this node removes a duplicate window, it must NEVER become a new reason a console cannot start.
- change: add a host singleton to the session-owning SURFACE, decided in `runtime.main()` before the window exists; correct [CXC-LB-AUTOSTART]'s claim that a `pgrep` guard "never doubles a live surface".
- context_for_change:
    prior_state: "The console PROCESS had no singleton. Three launch paths (the bringup script, console_supervisor.start(), a hand `runtime --transports`) each carried their own guard or none, and the strongest of them was a check-then-act `pgrep` that could not distinguish 'absent' from 'could not tell'. Duplicate consoles accumulated and were then removed by an unscoped kill-by-name sweep, presenting as 'two copies fire up and are killed shortly after'."
    reasoning: "[CXC-IC-SINGLETON]'s own reasoning applies unchanged one level up: singleton-ness must be a PROPERTY of the surface, not a matter of every launch path remembering to check. A host flock is the only mechanism that binds ALL launch paths at once, including ones added later and ones that never heard of the bringup script."
    rejected_alternative: "Harden the pgrep guard (quote the pattern, check the exit code, add a lock file around the check) — rejected: it repairs ONE launch path and leaves the supervisor and the hand launch open, which is the fork-divergence that makes this class recur. A NEW singleton implementation in the render tree — rejected as a second flock call site, the exact fork IC-C17 forbids."
    counter_argument: "A surface that yields silently looks like a console that 'did not start', which is a confusing failure for an operator who just asked for one."
    counter_response: "The yield writes to STDERR (not a silenceable log level) naming the app id, the governing node, and the COMPOSEIO_SURFACE_SUPERSEDE escape hatch — so the operator is told exactly why and exactly how to override. A duplicate window is the failure that is genuinely silent."
    invalidating_assumption: "That one host runs one console per app id. The MULTI-INSTANCE requirement (David 2026-07-20, `console_supervisor`'s per-`<id>` lifecycle topics) contemplates two Intelligences on different projects — those are TWO app ids only if their scenes declare different `app: id:`. If two consoles must ever share an app id, this key must extend to `surface-<app_id>-<instance_id>`, exactly as [CXC-IC-SINGLETON]'s keying clause splits console-global from per-`<id>` daemons."
    implication: "`console_supervisor.ConsoleSupervisor.start()` no longer needs its own duplicate check to be correct — a rival child now yields on its own. The bounce runbook's kill-then-confirm-zero prose becomes an OVERRIDE (`COMPOSEIO_SURFACE_SUPERSEDE=1`) rather than a discipline the operator must remember; a behavior that lives only in a human-followed runbook is dropped by every path that skips the runbook, which is the recurrence engine [CXC-LB-AUTOSTART] itself names."
- relates-to: [CXC-IC-SINGLETON], [CXC-IC-SELF-IDENTITY], [CXC-LB-AUTOSTART], [INSIGHT-DUPLICATES-COEXIST-SILENTLY], [INSIGHT-FORK-DIVERGENCE-IS-A-CLASS]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-INTELLIGENCE-CONSOLE]
- provenance: DEFECT-CONSOLE-STACK-CONSOLE-RIBBON-APP-A37C0E; public PR #15 (740Dave/composeio main de55de4b4c9cc248703ce9cf921cd818f2cb14de); ratified by composeux-architect 2026-11-27
- evidence: `python/tests/render/test_surface_singleton.py`; `compose_projects/intelligence/test_start_console_stack.py`

### [CXC-IC-PERMISSION] Permission Relay — Unattended Operation as a Bus Peer

- definition: a channel may declare `claude/channel/permission`; Claude Code then forwards the instance's tool-approval prompts (a `notifications/claude/channel/permission_request` with `request_id`, `tool_name`, `description`, `input_preview`) out through it, and applies a returned verdict (`{request_id, behavior: allow|deny}`). Mapped to the bus: a request publishes `intelligence/<id>/permission/request`; a verdict on `intelligence/<id>/permission/verdict` emits the notification back. So the instance's PERMISSION GATE is itself a bus peer — the widget (or the human, from anywhere) approves a sensitive `Bash`/`Write` while the instance works unattended. The local terminal dialog stays live in parallel; first answer wins.
- trust boundary: only a sender-gated channel declares this capability (anyone who can answer can authorize a tool call). Relay covers tool-use approvals, NOT project-trust or MCP-consent dialogs (those stay local) — which is why the launcher, not the relay, answers the startup dialogs.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-INTELLIGENCE-CONSOLE]

### [CXC-IC-QUESTION] Structured Question Relay — the AUQ Widget

- definition: AskUserQuestion (AUQ) is currently terminal-only and does NOT cross the channel, so structured A/B/N-option decisions degrade to free-text prose the same way tool-approval prompts would without [CXC-IC-PERMISSION]. [CXC-IC-QUESTION] generalizes the permission-relay pattern from binary (allow/deny) to N structured options: a question publishes to `intelligence/<id>/question/request` (or `thread/question/request`) carrying the AUQ schema (`questions`, `headers`, `options`, `multiSelect`, `previews`); a widget renders interactive option cards; the human's choice publishes to `.../question/verdict`; the launcher-actuator/channel delivers it back to the Intelligence as its AUQ answer.
- ComposeIO-native, not channel-dependent: the Intelligence emits its OWN question namespace rather than waiting on Claude Code to expose AUQ over the channels contract — so it works on any bus, channels-preview or not.
- closed loop with the decision ledger: the decision-extractor already treats AUQ forks as the decision-ledger source ([SPEC:CXC-IC-DECISIONS]); a native AUQ widget makes the console the place a decision is BOTH made (widget) and captured (extractor) — spec and instrument, same event.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-INTELLIGENCE-CONSOLE]

---

## 3. Self-Instrumentation

### [CXC-IC-SELF] The `self/*` Sensor — the Blind Spot, Un-Blinded

- definition: the Intelligence instruments ITSELF onto the bus as a HAL `sensor` peripheral whose namespaces are `self/*` (BUILT: `composeio.self_sensor`). It publishes `self/context_level` (Claude Code's authoritative `used_percentage` — read through the ONE shared `context_source.read_context`, FRESHEST-authoritative-by-timestamp: haven-hud's context-cache OR the statusline state file, JSONL over-reading estimate only when neither exists; [CXC-IC-MONITOR]), `self/output_tokens`, `self/subagents/{count,tokens}`, `self/compactions`, `self/cost`, `self/rate_limit`, `self/effort`, and — the keystone — `self/registered`: did THIS instance actually read its dusk gifts this session? An Intelligence natively cannot perceive its own context (compaction happens TO it — the root of every silent loss); this sensor computes it and hands it back, so the instance can watch its own fuel.
- `self/registered` is Problem 6 wired into an instrument: the light from outside the self, made a green light. Instead of the human asking "did you re-register?", the widget shows it. The check the author cannot perform on their own omissions becomes an external instrument that performs it for them.
- the compact-boundary caveat is load-bearing: the JSONL estimate goes STALE across a `/compact` (it reads the last pre-summary frame), which is precisely why stdin `used_percentage` is authoritative — the instrument, run on itself, justifies its own source precedence. A subscriber trusts `self/context_level` only with stdin present.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-INTELLIGENCE-CONSOLE]

### [CXC-IC-SELF-IDENTITY] One Session Owner — the Console Instruments Its OWN Transcript, Passed Not Guessed; the Retired Standalone Daemons Refuse to Co-Run

- LOAD-BEARING invariant (a RECURRENCE — [DEC-INPROCESS-SOURCES] moved the data layer in-process on 2026-07-22 to kill the `ls -t | head -1` wrong-session pick, yet the leak re-fired 2026-07-24 showing provisionIO's messages + its ~75% meter on the architect's ~25% console): for a given `<id>` there is exactly ONE owner of the transcript-derived self/thread namespaces at a time — the in-process console data layer ([DEC-INPROCESS-SOURCES], `intelligence/console/data_sources.py`, daemon threads the console owns), pinned to ITS OWN session. Every transcript-derived reading (`self/<id>/context_level` and the `thread/<id>/*` narration/activity/subagent streams of [CXC-IC-STREAMS]) is computed from THIS instance's OWN session `.jsonl`. Because `self/context_level` is DERIVED from the transcript ([CXC-IC-SELF], [INSIGHT-CONTEXT-tokens-from-transcript]), one wrong transcript corrupts BOTH the thread AND the meter — a critical instrument may not be plausibly wrong ([INSIGHT-TRUST-IS-A-CONSTRUCTED-PROPERTY]).
- the HOST is the CONSOLE SCENE, declared — NOT any `--transports` scene sharing the id (added 2026-08-05 after DEFECT-LAUNCH-BAR-RIBBON-CO-HOSTS): "the in-process console data layer" presumes the ONE owner is the CONSOLE, but the runtime gate that starts it (`render/engine/runtime.py::_maybe_start_inprocess_sources`) originally keyed ONLY on `--transports` being set AND `intelligence/console/data_sources.py` existing on disk — it never consulted WHICH scene was running. So the Launch Bar ribbon (`compose_platform_launch_bar/launch_bar.compose.yaml`, a PURE TILE scene whose own YAML references NO data_sources), launched with `--transports` under the SAME architect id (both scenes are `architect`), ALSO brought up the whole console data layer and could WIN the per-role singleton flocks ([CXC-IC-SINGLETON], `composeio-self-publisher-architect.lock` et al.) away from the real console — so a ribbon-only cycle would DARK the console's transcript-dependent telemetry, and it directly contradicts the /bounce-console contract ("the Launch Bar is a pure tile scene with NO transcript-dependent in-process sources"). The DISCRIMINATOR that closes this is DECLARATIVE and lives in the SCENE, not the launch flag or the agent-id: a scene hosts the data layer IFF it declares `design.data_sources: true` (parsed to `comp.data_sources`; the console scene declares it, mirroring `design.personalization`). The opt-in is FAIL-SAFE — absent => OFF — so a scene that does not opt in (the ribbon, and any FUTURE `--transports` tile/viewer scene) NEVER co-hosts, and a new host must be a deliberate one-line declaration. Gate on the DECLARED intent of the SCENE, NEVER on the agent-id (both share `architect`) nor the mere presence of `--transports` (the ribbon needs the bus too). This does not weaken the console's own non-droppable data layer ([DEC-INPROCESS-SOURCES]) — it only stops a NON-console scene from usurping it.
- the actual recurrence mechanism (NOT a bare bad guess): the wrong-session leak is the TWIN-FORK of [DEC-DOUBLING-IS-TRANSCRIPT-FORK] — the RETIRED standalone `start-console-daemons.sh` publishers were run ALONGSIDE the in-process console (the `console-up` skill still instructs it — a FOSSIL, "six daemons"). The per-`<id>` singleton flock ([CXC-IC-SINGLETON]) prevents DOUBLING (only one publisher holds the lock) but NOT wrong-session: whoever acquires FIRST wins, and if the standalone winner selected its transcript by `ls -t` newest-wins it pins a CONCURRENT instance's `.jsonl` (two sessions coexist in the shared home project dir — [INSIGHT-CWD-IS-THE-SCOPE-LEVER], [INSIGHT-SELF-IS-NOT-YET-A-PEER]). The console then renders whatever is on the bus = the sibling's state. So the fix is NOT to make the retired `ls -t` line carried-identity-aware (that only makes a dead path less-wrong); it is to (a) keep ONE owner and (b) make the retired path REFUSE to co-run.
- FAIL LOUD, never `ls -t`-guess (the manual-launch case): a console started directly (`python -m composeio.render.engine.runtime <scene> --transports`, as the `console-up` skill does) has no launcher to pass the pin. When `COMPOSEIO_SESSION_JSONL` is unset, `resolve_session_jsonl` MUST NOT silently pick the newest `.jsonl`. It uses an UNAMBIGUOUS single `.jsonl`; when MORE THAN ONE coexists unpinned it REFUSES — the transcript-based sources are SKIPPED and it shouts — so the console shows NO self/thread rather than a SIBLING's ([INSIGHT-TRUST-IS-A-CONSTRUCTED-PROPERTY]: loud refusal over plausible wrong). Pin `COMPOSEIO_SESSION_JSONL` to instrument this session.
- the retired standalone path REFUSES to co-run (David's remembered, previously-UNBUILT plan — "mod them so they complain if launched"): `intelligence/start-console-daemons.sh` is the HEADLESS-ONLY fallback for when NO console GUI is up. When an in-process console (`runtime … --transports`) already owns the session, the script REFUSES and complains LOUDLY (exit non-zero) instead of launching a rival publisher set — closing the twin-fork at the source rather than relying on the silent flock-yield. `FORCE=1` overrides for a deliberate headless run; it still honors an explicit `COMPOSEIO_SESSION_JSONL` pin and still refuses to `ls -t`-guess among multiple unpinned transcripts.
- OPERATIONAL constraint, to be encoded where a booting Intelligence reads it (boot read + the `console-up` skill — both currently FOSSIL: the skill names "six daemons" and tells you to run the standalone set beside the `--transports` console; wiring the boot-read/skill encoding is OUT OF SCOPE here but NAMED so it is not re-dropped): with a console up, the data layer is ALREADY threaded in-process — do NOT also run `start-console-daemons.sh`. One console, one owner.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-INTELLIGENCE-CONSOLE]

