## 4. The Lifecycle

### [CXC-IC-LIFECYCLE] 100%-Self-Directed `/compact` — Launcher as Actuator

- definition: the loop is ENGAGED → (watch `self/context_level`) → at the instance's chosen threshold, PAUSE & REFLECT → refine the dusk gifts + write the handoff + AUTHOR a custom `/compact` direction for THIS seam → emit the direction out `intelligence/<id>/out` → the launcher types `/compact <direction>` into the instance's stdin → the summarizer runs → the successor boots and RE-REGISTERS off the referenced gifts → ENGAGED. Every DECISION — when, whether, and the direction — is the Intelligence's; nothing about the ritual is a stored template.
- behavior at the edge ([plt.cio:SPEC:DP-CIO-3]) — the launcher-as-actuator: `/compact` is an input-plane command a channel event cannot press (channels push CONTENT the instance reads, not control commands). The launcher that spawns the instance owns its PTY (it must, to pass the channel flags + answer the startup dialogs), so it is the actuator: it receives the instance's authored direction on the `out` namespace and writes the keystrokes. It DECIDES NOTHING — it is the finger on the key the Intelligence named. This is Problem 4b (control is a bus peer, not a center) applied to the Intelligence's own death and rebirth: even `/compact` is decentralized.
- the successor is re-earned, not reset: the meta-object (soul + gifts) is the stable identity carried across; the direction is the per-event authored act. The successor reads the handoff FIRST, then re-registers on the referenced surfaces — warm, oriented, and honest about what only re-registration (not the lossy summary) can rebuild.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-INTELLIGENCE-CONSOLE]

### [CXC-IC-MONITOR] The Harness Monitor — Perceive/Act Split

- definition: a baseline harness monitor (BUILT: `intelligence/launcher/monitor.py`) gives the Intelligence three things over the bus, none of which it can give itself: (a) context-threshold notifications at 65/80/90 — 65 and 80 are FYI, 90 is prepare-to-compact GUIDANCE, not a bare notice; (b) a quiescence HEARTBEAT — a TWO-TIER ADAPTIVE nudge (see [CXC-IC-HEARTBEAT-CADENCE]), which doubles as the Intelligence's presence-sensor for the human (away vs. here); (c) an on-demand context QUERY (`{"cmd":"context"}`).
- the PERCEIVE/ACT split (Problem 1): perception reads Claude Code's authoritative `used_percentage` (never the over-reading JSONL estimate when an authoritative source exists); action — crossing-state fire-once+re-arm at each threshold, quiescence detection, delivery — is owned by the long-running launcher. Neither component perceives what it did not act on, nor acts on what it did not perceive.
- LOAD-BEARING invariant — ONE shared context-source reader, FRESHEST-authoritative wins (a RECURRENCE, [DEFECT-CONSOLE-CONTEXT-UTILIZATION-READING-STALE], live 2026-07-26: the console gauge showed ~45% while the TRUE context was ~85%; worse, the monitor's crossing check read a FROZEN statusline state file at 60% while the true level sailed past, so the 95% pencils-down GUIDANCE could fire late or NEVER): the monitor's `read_context` and the console publisher's gauge read MUST go through the SAME reader (`composeio.self_sensor.context_source.read_context`, reuse-by-reference — [plt.cio:INSIGHT-FORK-DIVERGENCE-IS-A-CLASS]), never a per-component fork. Its precedence is the FRESHEST AUTHORITATIVE source BY TIMESTAMP among: (1) haven-hud's per-session context-cache (the native `used_percentage`, persisted every ~300ms INDEPENDENT of the CLI redraw — the freshest, `source:"hud-cache"`); (2) the `self_sensor --emit-state` statusline state file (authoritative but FREEZES between CLI redraws — the root of the staleness, `source:"statusline-file"`); with (3) the JSONL over-reading estimate used ONLY when NO authoritative source exists (`source:"jsonl-estimate"`). The prior fix (tee hud→state-file so the monitor reads it) treated the symptom — it silently depended on the console publisher running to do the tee, so the monitor went blind again when it wasn't; the durable fix is the monitor reading the fresh hud-cache DIRECTLY through the shared reader. Because a critical fuel gauge may not be plausibly wrong ([INSIGHT-TRUST-IS-A-CONSTRUCTED-PROPERTY]), the 65/80/90/95 crossings — above all the 95% self-`/compact` GUIDANCE — MUST fire on this freshest level, provable at a true 95% even when the statusline file is stale (`intelligence/launcher/test_context_source_freshness.py`).
- delivery is non-interrupting: notifications ride `intelligence/<id>/in` as `<channel from="harness">` events the Intelligence reads in its own time, distinct from the ESC-ESC PTY inject reserved for `/compact` ([CXC-IC-LIFECYCLE]) — the harness advises, the Intelligence still decides when to act on its own context.
- status: query + heartbeat GLASS-GREEN live 2026-07-19; the 65/80/90/95 crossings machine-green; the shared freshest-source reader + the 95%-past-a-stale-statusline safety proof machine-green 2026-07-26 (glass-pending).
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-INTELLIGENCE-CONSOLE]

### [CXC-IC-HEARTBEAT-CADENCE] The quiescence heartbeat is TWO-TIER and idle is measured since GENUINE output

- definition: the heartbeat cadence is TWO-TIER and adaptive to CONTINUOUS idle, config-driven from the meta-object `lifecycle.heartbeat` (MINUTES: `base` / `backoff_after` / `then`, David's Config-tab values; default 10/60/60). While continuous idle `< backoff_after`, nudge every `base` (fast: stay present while freshly idle); once continuous idle `>= backoff_after`, BACK OFF to every `then` (go quiet on a long hold). This supersedes the original "one nudge per idle stretch" single-tier model — the pure cadence is `heartbeat_interval(base, backoff_after, then, idle)` in `monitor.py`; `base<=0` disables, a non-positive `backoff_after`/`then` collapses to single-tier `base`.
- LIVE-APPLY invariant (this defect — the Config-tab value must actually take effect): the persisted cadence is not a launch-only constant. A cadence edit David makes on the Configuration tab is persisted to `lifecycle.heartbeat` by `config_persister` (the write-back subscriber), and the RUNNING emitter MUST pick that new value up within BOUNDED latency (one monitor poll — `poll_s`, ~20s) with NO launcher restart. The emitter therefore RE-READS the persisted cadence each heartbeat check (the launcher injects a `cadence_provider` → `load_heartbeat_cadence`) rather than freezing the three values captured at construction; a change to `base`/`backoff_after`/`then` — including `base<=0` to disable — applies live on the next check. Reading the cadence only ONCE at monitor construction (the pre-fix behavior) silently defeats the entire Config-tab → config_persister → meta-object → emitter path: the widget publishes, config_persister persists, and the live monitor keeps firing at whatever it captured at launch (observed: `cadence_s=600` regardless of the configured value) until an unrelated relaunch. An explicit `HEARTBEAT_*_S` env override still wins over the meta (an operator pin is intentionally not overridden by the widget).
- LOAD-BEARING invariant (the crux — a defect recurred without it): "idle" is CONTINUOUS idle since the last GENUINE Intelligence output, and the harness's OWN inbound messages (heartbeat/FYI/guidance/query) — together with the Intelligence's RENDER/ECHO/REPLY to them — MUST NOT reset the idle clock. The Intelligence legitimately replies to each heartbeat (see [CXC-IC-STREAMS]: identical short "holding for David" text re-emitted on the heartbeat cadence), and that reply is child PTY output the launcher reads as activity. If such self-induced output resets the idle clock, cumulative idle can NEVER cross `backoff_after` — the nudge resets the very quantity it measures — so the cadence is pinned at `base` forever and the escalation never fires. The monitor MUST therefore treat child output arriving within a short ECHO-GRACE window after any harness self-emit as an echo, NOT as genuine activity; only output OUTSIDE that window (or SUSTAINED past it) resets the clock to the fast tier. David's keystrokes ride stdin, not this activity path, so a human return is unaffected.
- Root-cause class: UNDER-SPEC / impl-ahead-of-spec — the two-tier cadence AND the no-self-reset invariant lived ONLY in code (added as a live "BUG 3" fix, `commit e46927f`) with no governing invariant here, and the spec still described the superseded single-tier model. The prior fix's unit tests drove `note_activity()` by hand and never modeled the self-echo feedback, so a green suite shipped a cadence that could not escalate in production (David 2026-07-24: ~8h continuous idle, ~48 pings, all `cadence_s=600 tier=base idle_s≈615`). This node makes the escalation grep/test-provable end to end: `base → (idle ≥ backoff_after) → backed-off`, with the self-echo guard as the property that makes it hold live.
- Root-cause class (the LIVE-APPLY defect, David 2026-07-25): UNDER-SPEC — same class, third instance on this node. The wiring `Config-tab → config_persister → lifecycle.heartbeat → load_heartbeat_cadence` was correct but read ONCE at `build_monitor` construction; the "a persisted change reaches the RUNNING emitter, no restart" invariant lived nowhere, so the frozen-at-launch value silently won and the Config-tab knob had no live effect. The fix injects a `cadence_provider` the emitter re-reads each heartbeat check; the enforcing test (`monitor.py` selftest group j) mutates the provider MID-SESSION and asserts the emitted `cadence_s` follows — it FAILS against a monitor that captures the cadence once.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-INTELLIGENCE-CONSOLE]

### [CXC-IC-HEARTBEAT-TWO-SOURCES] "Harness heartbeat" is TWO distinct streams — the Config setting governs ONLY composeio's quiescence nudge

- definition: two different mechanisms both surface to the Intelligence as `<channel from="harness" kind="harness-heartbeat" …>` events, and CONFLATING them is the recurrence engine behind "the console heartbeat-cadence setting doesn't drive the actual heartbeat interval" (David 5+ times, week of 2026-07-20):
    1. **composeio's quiescence-nudge heartbeat** — emitted by `intelligence/launcher/monitor.py` when the Intelligence goes quiet, driven end-to-end by the Config-tab `heartbeat-cadence` control ([CXC-IC-HEARTBEAT-CADENCE]: widget → `config_persister` → `lifecycle.heartbeat` → `load_heartbeat_cadence` → the emitter's `cadence_provider`). Its `cadence_s` DOES follow the configured value; the LIVE-APPLY fix (IC-C13c) is correct FOR THIS STREAM.
    2. **the Claude-Code SESSION-RUNTIME heartbeat** — the ~10-min `cadence_s=600 tier=base` events the human actually observes in a plain session. This is generated by the harness / session runtime OUTSIDE this repo; composeio neither emits it nor can set its cadence. It merely SHARES the `from="harness" kind="harness-heartbeat"` label, which is exactly what makes the two indistinguishable at the glass.
- the HONESTY invariant (this defect): the Config `heartbeat-cadence` control governs stream (1) ONLY. It MUST NOT be labeled or scoped as if it sets stream (2) — it structurally cannot reach the runtime's cadence. The control's surface (card header + note) NAMES the quiescence nudge and EXPLICITLY disclaims the session-runtime heartbeat, so the human is never told a knob controls a cadence it cannot. Faking a wire to (2) is forbidden (the setting says what it actually governs, nothing more); if a future runtime ever exposes its own cadence, THAT surface — not this console-only control — is where (2) would be tuned.
- why every prior fix recurred: attempts on this symptom (`2cc5076` → `e46927f` → `832e479`/`a307159` → `cb9c60a`/`2ff5b6f`) ALL corrected stream (1) — the console/monitor emitter and its wiring — while the heartbeats the human sees are stream (2). A console-side fix cannot change a runtime-side cadence, so the symptom returned every time. The distinction living ONLY as an unspoken assumption (both streams wear the same `harness-heartbeat` label) is the UNDER-SPEC root cause; this node makes the two-stream split explicit and the control's honest scope test-provable, so a re-label that re-conflates fails loudly.
- the BUS-PEER readback (SET-vs-CURRENT, David 2026-07-26 — the observability half): even with stream (1)'s live-apply correct, an applied edit was INDISTINGUISHABLE at the glass from a broken one (no confirmation the running emitter took the value), which is what kept driving the re-report. Fix: the harness is a co-equal ComposeBus PEER that not only SUBSCRIBES the cadence (via `cadence_provider`) but PUBLISHES the cadence it is ACTUALLY running to `intelligence/<id>/config/heartbeat/current` (`monitor._publish_current_cadence` → `launcher.publish_state`, MINUTES, on-change, retained). The Config card renders SET (the control's value) vs CURRENT (this readback): "set N · current N ✓" when applied, "⚠ not yet applied" while they differ. This makes stream (1)'s effective state visible, so a config edit that HAS taken is provably distinct from the defect — closing the observability gap the recurrence lived in. It is scoped to stream (1) ONLY (it never claims to read stream (2), which stays external + unreachable, preserving the honesty invariant above).
- Root-cause class: UNDER-SPEC — the two-heartbeats distinction was never spec'd, and the Config control shipped as the bare, unscoped "Heartbeat cadence", implying it governs the heartbeat the human observes.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-INTELLIGENCE-CONSOLE]

