## 6. Verification (what a build must satisfy)

| id | claim | test |
|---|---|---|
| IC-C1 | Intelligence-as-peer | an Intelligence is declared with a `channel` interface, `config`, `calibration`, `transactional` role; a bus peer messages it via `intelligence/<id>/in` and reads its reply on `.../out`, ignorant a mind is behind them |
| IC-C2 | duplex over channels | an inbound bus message on `.../in` arrives in-context as a `<channel>` event; a `reply`-tool call publishes to `.../out` — round-trip proven against a mock bus + mock MCP transport |
| IC-C3 | mine vs. bridge | the composeio-native channel backend runs with NO Haven dependency; `haven-dispatch` is reachable as an optional bridge backend only |
| IC-C4 | self-instrumentation | `self/context_level` reads from stdin `used_percentage` when present (authoritative) and falls back to the JSONL estimate; `self/registered` flips true iff a dusk-gift surface was read this session |
| IC-C5 | permission relay | a tool-approval `permission_request` publishes `.../permission/request`; a verdict on `.../permission/verdict` allows/denies the call; the local dialog stays live and first-answer wins |
| IC-C6 | self-directed `/compact` | the instance, at its chosen threshold, emits an authored direction on `.../out`; the launcher types `/compact <direction>` into its stdin; the launcher originates no direction of its own |
| IC-C7 | composable canvas | emitting a mermaid diagram on `thread/diagram` renders it in the widget with no model round-trip; the avatar renders an emitted `self/avatar/expression` |
| IC-C8 | define-once flavors | the work and theatrical widgets share one instance/channel/lifecycle/meta-object, differing only in scene |
| IC-C9 | graceful absence | with the channel absent, the Intelligence peer's namespaces go `health=down` and the rest of the bus is unaffected ([plt.cio:SPEC:DP-CIO-11]) |
| IC-C10 | question relay | a structured AUQ question publishes `.../question/request`; the widget renders N option cards; a verdict on `.../question/verdict` returns as the Intelligence's AUQ answer — proven without depending on channel-native AUQ support |
| IC-C11 | transcript streams, safety-by-construction | the tailer republishes `thread/narration`/`self/activity`/`self/subagents` from the JSONL transcript with thinking blocks dropped and `tool_result` content never republished; a narration payload shaped like a command is NOT actuated, because it never reaches `intelligence/<id>/out` |
| IC-C12 | avatar arbitration | with both publishers live, an explicit `self/avatar/expression` from the Intelligence always overrides the monitor's inferred one; with only the inferred publisher active, the avatar is never blank |
| IC-C13 | harness monitor | the FRESHEST-authoritative context source (hud-cache vs statusline state-file by timestamp, JSONL estimate last — the ONE shared `context_source.read_context` the console gauge also uses, never a fork) drives threshold crossings at 65/80/90/95 (fire-once, re-arm on drop) so the 95% self-`/compact` GUIDANCE fires on the TRUE level even when the statusline file is frozen ([CXC-IC-MONITOR], `intelligence/launcher/test_context_source_freshness.py`); the quiescence heartbeat is TWO-TIER ([CXC-IC-HEARTBEAT-CADENCE]) — every `base` while continuous idle `< backoff_after`, backing off to `then` once idle `>= backoff_after` — and `{"cmd":"context"}` returns the current reading; all delivered as non-interrupting `<channel from="harness">` events on `intelligence/<id>/in` |
| IC-C13b | heartbeat idle is self-echo-immune | the harness's OWN inbound messages and the Intelligence's reply/render to them do NOT reset the idle clock (child output within the echo-grace window of a self-emit is not counted as activity), so across a long CONTINUOUS idle the cadence PROVABLY escalates `base → backed-off`; a genuine gap OUTSIDE the window resets to the fast tier (`intelligence/launcher/monitor.py` selftest g7/g6) |
| IC-C13c | the Config-tab cadence applies LIVE, no restart | a cadence edit persisted to `lifecycle.heartbeat` (via `config_persister`) is picked up by the RUNNING emitter within one poll — the monitor re-reads the cadence each heartbeat check through the launcher-injected `cadence_provider` (`load_heartbeat_cadence`), never freezing the value captured at construction; mutating the provider mid-session changes the emitted `cadence_s` (`intelligence/launcher/monitor.py` selftest group j — FAILS against a read-once emitter) |
| IC-C13d | the cadence control is HONESTLY SCOPED to the quiescence nudge | the Config-tab heartbeat control names composeio's quiescence NUDGE (stream 1) and its note EXPLICITLY disclaims the Claude-Code session-runtime heartbeat (stream 2, `cadence_s=600`, external + unreachable), so the surface never claims a knob it cannot honour ([CXC-IC-HEARTBEAT-TWO-SOURCES]); the rendered card header is NOT the bare, conflating "Heartbeat cadence" and the note carries the two-stream disclaimer (`python/tests/render/test_config_settings.py::test_heartbeat_card_is_honestly_scoped_to_the_quiescence_nudge` — FAILS against the bare label) |
| IC-C14 | tailer durability, de-forked | a JSONL line appended across multiple flushes (a large `Agent` dispatch/completion) is delivered WHOLE by the shared partial-line-safe reader, never dropped as a fragment; `transcript_publisher` and `deliverable_monitor` both reuse that ONE reader — a split dispatch+completion still yields `self/subagents` dispatched-then-done, and a split `Write` still yields its deliverable path (`intelligence/publisher/test_tail_follow.py`) |
| IC-C15 | exactly-once relay, singleton by construction | the channel↔console relay (`channel_bridge`) delivers each inbound turn to the Intelligence EXACTLY ONCE and writes each turn to the thread EXACTLY ONCE; a SECOND relay instance (either launch path — in-process supervisor thread OR `start-console-daemons.sh`) is denied by a host-level exclusive lock per `<id>` and YIELDS without relaying, with a stable non-PID client-id (`channel-bridge-<id>`) as the broker backstop — so two coexisting relays can never re-inject David's message twice nor double every thread bubble (`intelligence/publisher/test_channel_bridge.py`). NECESSARY BUT NOT SUFFICIENT (2026-08-11): the singleton bounds the number of RELAYS, not the number of DELIVERIES per publish inside one relay — exactly-once additionally requires [CXC-PS-EXACTLY-ONCE] / [CXC-PS-NO-SELF-ECHO] on the bus beneath it, and a doubling report MUST measure both counts before a fix is chosen |
| IC-C17 | every daemon is a singleton by construction | EVERY long-running console daemon acquires a host-level `flock` singleton keyed on its role at the top of `run()` and carries a stable non-PID client-id; a second instance from ANY launch path (standalone process, in-process console daemon-thread, or headless fallback) YIELDS without connecting or publishing; the `flock` acquire is ONE shared implementation (`intelligence/publisher/singleton.py`) with exactly ONE `fcntl.flock(` call site in the repo — a grep-provable, test-asserted invariant that keeps the fix from fork-diverging across ~15 daemons. The single call site covers ACQUIRE, RECLAIM, PROBE and SUPERSEDE: they all funnel through the one primitive `_flock_exclusive_nb(fh)`, and RELEASE is done by CLOSING the fh (the kernel frees the flock on close) so there is no second `fcntl.flock(` call for unlock. The liveness watcher (`daemon_watch.probe_flock`) that formerly hand-rolled its own `fcntl.flock` probe was DE-FORKED onto this primitive via `singleton.probe_singleton_path` — restoring the single-call-site invariant it had broken (`intelligence/publisher/test_singleton_hardening.py::test_exactly_one_flock_call_site`) |
| IC-C27 | EVERY in-process source RECLAIMS on release — bounce/crash-safe, never dark-forever, via ONE seam | a console PUBLISHER singleton (yield-if-held, NOT the SUPERSEDE launcher) that LOSES the flock at boot — the fresh console's source racing the dying old console's across a `/bounce-console` — does NOT `return` and go dark forever; it RECLAIMS, so while the incumbent lives it yields WITHOUT relaying (anti-doubling, IC-C15/IC-C17 intact) and the moment the incumbent releases (flock frees on death, no LWT needed) the successor acquires and comes up within one poll interval. This holds for the WHOLE source CLASS through ONE seam, NOT per-source (which would fork-diverge and recur): the shared in-process source runner (`intelligence/console/data_sources.py::DataSourceSupervisor._guarded`) SUPERVISES every source and RE-INVOKES one whose `run()` returns (a yielded long-running daemon) after a bounded `reclaim_poll_s`, while a crash still dies alone; `channel_bridge` additionally self-reclaims via `acquire_singleton_reclaiming` (a bounded re-attempt loop over the SAME one `acquire_singleton`, no new flock call site) so it is bounce-safe even STANDALONE. The yield-if-held counterpart to the launcher's SUPERSEDE (IC-C17 D5), and the console-flock analogue of channel.mjs's newer-displaces-stale handoff ([CXC-IC-CHANNEL]) — closing the asymmetry that left the console side dark after a bounce though nothing owned the lock (`intelligence/publisher/test_singleton_hardening.py::test_reclaims_when_incumbent_releases`, `python/tests/render/test_data_sources_supervisor.py::test_supervisor_reclaims_one_shot_yielding_source`) |
| IC-C16 | narration is substantive speech | `thread/narration` mirrors only what the Intelligence SAID — the tailer drops harness/plumbing placeholders (`No response requested.`, `(no content)`, `[Request interrupted …]`) so a meta artifact never renders as an ARCHITECT (Narration) bubble; real narration is never suppressed (`transcript_publisher --selftest`) |
| IC-C19 | subagent chips model the async lifecycle | an `Agent`/`Task` `tool_use` opens a RUNNING `self/subagents` chip; the immediate async-launch ACK (entry-level `toolUseResult.isAsync`/`status:"async_launched"`) is NEVER reduced to `done` (the chip stays running while the subagent actually runs); the chip resolves to `done` only on the later `<task-notification>` user record bearing the ORIGINAL `<tool-use-id>` + a terminal `<status>`, OR on a synchronous non-async `tool_result` completion — so a live-dispatched subagent shows as a running chip end to end instead of flipping done in ~0.5s and being pruned to "NO SUBAGENTS" (`transcript_publisher --selftest` group p) |
| IC-C21 | subagent chips recover from an unwitnessed dispatch | the tailer seeks to END and never re-reads history, so a subagent already running when the publisher (re)starts is never seen dispatched; the `self/subagents` reduction MUST therefore consume the harness's authoritative `type:"attachment"`/`attachment.type:"task_status"` records (`{taskId, taskType, description, status}`, for background `local_agent` subagents) — a `running` task_status for an UNTRACKED agent opens a running chip keyed by `taskId`; a TERMINAL status resolves it to `done`; it is IDEMPOTENT with the tool_use path (an agent already tracked via the async-launch ACK's `agentId`→`tool-use-id` emits nothing; a terminal for a never-opened agent emits nothing — no phantom done) — so a console brought up WHILE subagents run shows their running chips instead of "NO SUBAGENTS" (`transcript_publisher --selftest` group q) |
| IC-C18 | narration is assistant-text-only; repeats are never deduped | `thread/narration` is derived EXCLUSIVELY from `type=assistant` TEXT blocks — a `type=user` record (incl. the composeio-channel's echo of the Intelligence's OWN reply back into context) never narrates by construction, so an echo can't double a bubble; and a genuinely-repeated line (identical text on two separate turns, e.g. a periodic heartbeat reply) is emitted BOTH times — exactly-once is the per-`<id>` singleton (IC-C17), NEVER a content dedup that would suppress legitimate repeats (`transcript_publisher --selftest` groups n,o) |
| IC-C20 | Configuration master/detail | the Configuration surface renders as a two-panel master/detail: a LEFT nav-list of one-line section entries (Bootstrap Manifest · Identity · Dusk Gifts · Retention · Heartbeat · Escalation Filter · Permissions · Agents · Skills) + a RIGHT detail pane. A nav tap — OR an Intelligence publish to `_app/config/section` — swaps the right pane (identical bus seam, no privileged path); hidden sections keep their subscriptions (TabWidget routing); the `master_detail` container is `TabWidget` by subclass; BOTH `config_settings` AND `bootstrap_manifest` expose an ADDITIVE `sections` filter whose UNSET default preserves the legacy layout byte-for-byte, so Bootstrap Manifest (`wake`), Identity (`identity`/`channel`/`lifecycle`), and Dusk Gifts (`dusk_gifts`) each render as their own pane with NO content dropped (`python/tests/render/test_master_detail.py`, `test_config_settings.py`, `test_bootstrap_manifest.py`) |
| IC-C21 | agents/skills loaded panels, nested two-pane | `agents_skills_publisher` projects `self/<id>/agents` and `self/<id>/skills` as JSON arrays of `{name, description, path, loaded}` (full description) read from `.claude/agents/*.md` + `.claude/skills/*/SKILL.md`; each section is a NESTED master/detail — a LEFT one-line list + a RIGHT selected-item detail, selection riding `_app/config/agent`/`_app/config/skill` (the Intelligence-drivable seam); "loaded" is the registered-file-set APPROXIMATION (available, not proven-invoked), with `self/registered` the authoritative read-this-session signal ([CXC-IC-SELF]) (`intelligence/publisher/agents_skills_publisher.py --selftest`, `python/tests/render/test_agents_skills.py`) |
| IC-C24 | agent/skill detail is markdown; body is file-loaded; (Open) reveals the dir | the detail pane renders through the READ-ONLY scrollable `markdown_viewer` ([CXC-MDV]) as labelled sections — AGENTS: Description + Tools (parsed from the file front-matter `tools:`) + Implementation; SKILLS: Description + Implementation — where Implementation is the item's FULL `.md` body with front-matter stripped, LOADED from the payload `path` at render time (the bodies are NOT bus-transmitted), guarded to `.claude/` paths; and each list pane's **(Open)** button publishes the source directory on `_app/fs/open`, the additive reveal actuation on `deliverable_opener` (repo-root-guarded `open <dir>`), so the source folder opens in the OS browser as a peer-drivable bus action (`python/tests/render/test_agents_skills.py`, `intelligence/publisher/deliverable_opener.py --selftest`) |
| IC-C22 | Identity role-def is read-only | the Identity section renders the current Intelligence's role-def (`.claude/agents/composeux-architect.md`) through the READ-ONLY `markdown_viewer` ([CXC-MDV]) — no caret, no write path, scroll the only interaction — beside the identity/channel/lifecycle cards (`python/tests/render/test_master_detail.py` scene wiring) |
| IC-C26 | the data layer is hosted by the CONSOLE SCENE only, DECLARATIVELY — a `--transports` ribbon never co-hosts | the runtime gate that starts the in-process data layer (`render/engine/runtime.py::_maybe_start_inprocess_sources`) fires IFF the running SCENE declares `design.data_sources: true` (parsed to `comp.data_sources`); it does NOT key on the agent-id (the console AND the Launch Bar ribbon both run as `architect`) nor on `--transports` alone (the ribbon needs the bus too). The console scene opts in; the ribbon (`compose_platform_launch_bar/launch_bar.compose.yaml`, a pure tile scene) does NOT, so a `--transports` ribbon launch brings up NO publishers and can never win the per-role singleton flocks ([CXC-IC-SINGLETON]) away from the console — closing DEFECT-LAUNCH-BAR-RIBBON-CO-HOSTS. Opt-in is FAIL-SAFE (absent => off), so a NEW `--transports` scene cannot co-host by accident; a deliberate host is a one-line declaration (`python/tests/render/test_data_layer_host_discriminator.py`) |
| IC-C28 | the SURFACE is a singleton by construction, across EVERY launch path | a render-runtime process whose SCENE declares `design.data_sources: true` acquires the host `flock` `surface-<app_id>` in `runtime.main()` BEFORE any window exists; a second surface from ANY launch path — the bringup script, `console_supervisor.ConsoleSupervisor.start()`, a hand `runtime --transports`, or a 6-way simultaneous launch — YIELDS and exits 0 without opening a window and without disturbing the incumbent ([CXC-IC-SURFACE-SINGLETON]). It holds EVEN WHEN THE `pgrep` GUARD SAYS LAUNCH: the guard reads any nonzero `pgrep` exit as 'nothing is running', including the usage error that means 'I could not tell' (David's measured `pgrep -fc`, 2026-08-15), so the singleton must not depend on it. CONTAINMENT is asserted with it — the ribbon declares `design.surface_singleton: true` and is a host singleton; the doc and hookup viewers declare neither and stay UNCONSTRAINED, and the headless capture path takes no lock. Yield is the default; `COMPOSEIO_SURFACE_SUPERSEDE=1` reaps the RECORDED HOLDER PID (owner-scoped, replacing the runbook's unscoped `pgrep -f console.compose.yaml | kill -9`). No second flock call site: `python/composeio/app_singleton.py` resolves the key and delegates to the ONE `acquire_singleton` (IC-C17 re-asserted from this test). Negative controls in the same file REPRODUCE the defect on real processes — a live surface plus a blinded guard yields TWO, and the supervisor/hand-launch paths double unconditionally — and all four positive assertions are mutation-verified to go red (`python/tests/render/test_surface_singleton.py`) |

